technical_analysis:
A PureDB (virtual-user) record is one line of fields separated by PW_LINE_SEP (`:`). The daemon reads it positionally in pw_puredb_parseline(): position 13 is the allowed local IP, 14 denied local IP, 15 allowed client IP, 16 denied client IP and 17 the time restrictions. pure_strtok2() in src/utils.c performs a plain single-character split with no quoting or escaping, and the writer add_new_pw_line() in src/pure-pw.c prints each pattern with `%s` without validation:

```c title=src/pure-pw.c startLineNumber=711 endLineNumber=731
    if (pwinfo->allow_local_ip != NULL) {
        fprintf(fp2, "%s", pwinfo->allow_local_ip);   /* allowed local ip */
    }
    ...
    if (pwinfo->deny_client_ip != NULL) {
        fprintf(fp2, "%s", pwinfo->deny_client_ip);   /* denied client ip */
    }
```

With `-R '::1/128'` the two colons of the literal add two separators, so the deny column at position 16 is empty and `1/128` is pushed to position 18, which the parser never reads. access_ip_check() therefore sees an empty deny list and returns success for every address; pure-pw show also reports an empty deny list, so the misconfiguration is invisible to the operator. With `-r '::1/128'` (allow list) the fragment lands in the time-restriction column, time_restrictions_check() cannot parse it and returns -1, so every login is denied while the allow restriction is also gone.

The IPv6 CIDR matcher in access_ip_match() (ipv6_cidr_match() plus inet_pton(AF_INET6, ...)) is correct but cannot be reached with an IPv6 literal through the product's only rule-writing interface, because such a literal can never occupy a single colon-separated field. IPv6 hostname rules contain no colon and do work.

code_locations.fix_after:
    if (time_restrictions_check(time_restrictions) != 0) {
        return -1;
    }
    if (pure_strtok2(&cursor, *PW_LINE_SEP) != NULL) {
        return -1;              /* malformed record: unexpected trailing fields */
    }

    return 0;
}

if (fp2 == NULL) {
    return -1;
}
if ((pwinfo->allow_local_ip != NULL &&
     strchr(pwinfo->allow_local_ip, *PW_LINE_SEP) != NULL) ||
    (pwinfo->deny_local_ip != NULL &&
     strchr(pwinfo->deny_local_ip, *PW_LINE_SEP) != NULL) ||
    (pwinfo->allow_client_ip != NULL &&
     strchr(pwinfo->allow_client_ip, *PW_LINE_SEP) != NULL) ||
    (pwinfo->deny_client_ip != NULL &&
     strchr(pwinfo->deny_client_ip, *PW_LINE_SEP) != NULL)) {
    fputs("IP access rules cannot contain the ':' field separator;\n"
          "use a hostname rule for IPv6 clients instead of an address "
          "literal.\n", stderr);
    return -1;
}

technical_analysis:
The IPv6 branch of the EPRT parser declares the address structure without initializing it:

```c title=src/ftpd.c startLineNumber=838 endLineNumber=866
static void do_ipv6_port(char *p, char delim)
{
    char *deb;
    struct sockaddr_storage a;        /* uninitialized */
    ...
    if (generic_aton(deb, &a) != 0) {
        goto nope;
    }
    doport2(a, (unsigned int) port);
}
```

`generic_aton()` only sets the family (`ss_family`) and the 16 address bytes (`sin6_addr`); `sin6_flowinfo` and `sin6_scope_id` are left with whatever the stack contains. `doport2()` then compares the address with the control connection peer and, when they differ and FXP is not allowed, formats both addresses with `getnameinfo(..., NI_NUMERICHOST)`. glibc's IPv6 formatter appends `%<sin6_scope_id>` whenever the scope id is non-zero, so the uninitialized word is rendered as a decimal suffix in the `500` reply to the client.

The IPv4 EPRT branch (`memset(&a, 0, sizeof a);` before filling) and `doport()` already do this correctly, which confirms the omission is specific to the IPv6 helper.

code_locations.fix_after:
memset(&a, 0, sizeof a);
if (generic_aton(deb, &a) != 0) {
    goto nope;
}
